\description{
ROC analyses of two-choice data with a confidence rating, after Weidemann and
Kahana (2016), with their areas (AUC) and, for posterior predictives, the
same per posterior draw with a median and interval. \code{croc()} gives the
confidence ROC (C-ROC) and the zROC, \code{lroc()} the latency ROC (L-ROC), from
response-time quantiles, and \code{rroc()} the response-contingent ROCs (C-RROC,
L-RROC, optionally within one confidence level).
}
\details{
The conventions are those of Weidemann and Kahana (2016) as used by
Reynolds, Osth, Kvam and Heathcote for the multiple-threshold race (MTR;
their Figure 2 and analysis code); AUCs are comparable with those papers
only under them.
\itemize{
\item \strong{Signal and noise.} Trials with \code{signal_factor == noise} are noise
trials and every other level is a signal level. Each signal level is
analysed against the noise trials (one curve per level). The signal
response is \code{signal_response}: by default the level of \code{R} named like the
(single) signal level, otherwise the second level of \code{R}.
\item \strong{C-ROC.} Hit rate (HR, signal trials) against false-alarm rate (FAR,
noise trials), cumulated from the most confident signal response down to
the least confident, then from the least confident noise response up to
the most confident: \verb{2K + 1} points from (0, 0) to (1, 1) for \code{K} ratings
(column \code{response} names the response added at each point). AUC by
trapezoids. The random ROC
joins (0, 0), the classification point (point \code{K}, after all signal
responses) and (1, 1); its area \code{rAUC} depends on accuracy alone and the
confidence resolution is \code{cRes = cAUC - rAUC}.
\item \strong{zROC.} The probits of the \verb{2K - 1} interior points (NA where a
proportion is 0 or 1), with the least-squares slope \code{zSlope} and intercept
\code{zInt} of z(HR) on z(FAR), and \code{zCurv}, the quadratic coefficient of a
least-squares quadratic (negative = convex, i.e. bent towards the lower
right). \code{\link[=zroc]{zroc()}} gives the same points in the opposite order.
\item \strong{L-ROC.} Rating is replaced by RT: the RTs of each response are cut at
their own \code{n_quantiles} quantiles (type 7, pooled over signal and noise
trials), giving \code{n_quantiles - 1} bins per response. The curve runs
through the signal responses from the fastest bin to the slowest, then
through the noise responses from the slowest to the fastest, \verb{2 n_quantiles - 1} points; \code{lAUC}, the random ROC through point
\code{n_quantiles - 1} (\code{lrAUC}) and the latency resolution \code{lRes}.
\item \strong{Response-contingent ROCs.} One response at a time, HR and FAR
cumulated within that response: confidence (\code{cAUC_sig}, \code{cAUC_noise};
signal responses from high to low confidence, noise responses from low to
high, \code{K + 1} points) or latency (\code{lAUC_sig}, \code{lAUC_noise}; signal
responses fastest first, noise responses slowest first, \code{n_quantiles}
points). With \code{conf}, the latency version within each given confidence
level (\verb{lAUC_sig_c<k>}, \verb{lAUC_noise_c<k>}), with the RT bins recomputed
within the level. A response's AUC is below .5 when its slow (signal) or
fast (noise) trials are the more accurate.
\item \strong{Pooling and bounds.} Without \code{by}, trials are pooled over everything
else, the subject factor included (as \code{\link[=zroc]{zroc()}}); \code{by = "subjects"} (or
any factors) gives one set per level. Censored trials (\code{R} or \code{RR} NA, as
coded by \code{\link[=make_missing]{make_missing()}}) are dropped, and for the latency ROCs also
trials without a finite RT. \code{bounds} keeps only trials with \verb{LC <= rt <= UC} (the rule of \code{\link[=make_missing]{make_missing()}}) in the data and the predictions alike,
for a comparison within a censoring window. Quantile bins are taken from
the trials pooled within a \code{by} level (and within a draw for the
predictions).
}

A shared don't-know response (\code{dont_know}) is placed between the signal and
noise responses of the C-ROC (\verb{2K} points), which then has no classification
point (\code{rAUC} and \code{cRes} NA); \code{lroc()} and \code{rroc()} do not handle it.
}
\examples{
# simulated old/new recognition data with three confidence levels
sim <- function(n) {
  S <- factor(rep(c("new", "old"), each = n / 2))
  e <- rnorm(n, ifelse(S == "old", 1, 0), ifelse(S == "old", 1.25, 1))
  R <- factor(ifelse(e > .5, "old", "new"), levels = c("new", "old"))
  RR <- as.integer(cut(abs(e - .5), c(0, .4, 1, Inf)))
  data.frame(S = S, R = R, RR = RR, rt = .3 + rexp(n, 1 + 2 * abs(e - .5)))
}
set.seed(1)
dat <- sim(1000)
pp <- do.call(rbind, lapply(1:20, function(i) cbind(postn = i, sim(1000))))
x <- croc(dat, "S", "new", post_predict = pp)
x$stats
plot_lroc(lroc(dat, "S", "new", post_predict = pp))
rroc(dat, "S", "new", type = "latency", conf = 3)$stats
}
